      {
        "id": 4301575573,
        "node_id": "PR_kwDOJlIj0c8AAAABAGTVlQ",
        "number": 1541,
        "state": "closed",
        "title": "feat: add an on-device model backed by Chrome built-in AI",
        "body": "## What\n\nAdds a **Built-in AI** entry to the model selector, which runs the chat on-device using the Chrome/Edge built-in AI APIs instead of the gateway. It only appears on browsers that expose those APIs; everywhere else the selector is byte-for-byte what it is today.\n\nIt's backed by [`@browser-ai/core`](https://browser-ai.dev/), an AI SDK community provider ([listed on ai-sdk.dev](https://ai-sdk.dev/providers/community-providers), and the subject of the Chrome for Developers post [Use the Prompt API with the Vercel AI SDK](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/prompt-api-with-vercel-ai)). Disclosure: I maintain it.\n\n## Why\n\nThe template is the reference for \"how do I build this with the AI SDK,\" and in-browser inference is the one provider shape it can't currently demonstrate — not because the AI SDK can't do it, but because the whole chat path assumes a server round-trip. This shows the seam where that assumption can be relaxed, and it's genuinely useful on its own: no key, no gateway spend, and no prompt text leaving the device.\n\n## How\n\nThree pieces, all additive:\n\n- **`ModelAwareChatTransport`** (`lib/ai/browser-transport.ts`) chooses between the existing `DefaultChatTransport` and a browser transport per request. `useChat` reads its transport once, so the switch has to happen inside the transport rather than by swapping instances. The gateway path is untouched — same `prepareSendMessagesRequest`, same body, same `/api/chat`.\n- **Capability-gated selector entry.** `useBrowserAISupport` checks after mount (the server render has no `LanguageModel` global), so nothing changes for unsupported browsers and there's no hydration mismatch.\n- **`POST /api/chat/local`** persists the exchange once generation finishes, since the stream never reaches the server and `/api/chat` therefore never writes it. It reuses the same auth check, IP rate limit, entitlement check, `saveChat`/`saveMessages`, and title generation as `/api/chat` rather than introducing a second persistence story.\n\n## Scope and trade-offs\n\nThe on-device path is text only: no tools, no artifacts, no resumable streams. None of those are reachable without a server round-trip, so it's scoped down rather than the gateway models being degraded to match. `reconnectToStream` returns `null` for the same reason.\n\nAdds one dependency (`@browser-ai/core`), client-side only.\n\n`tsc --noEmit`, `biome check`, and `next build` all pass; `/api/chat/local` shows up in the route table.\n\nHappy to split the persistence route out, gate the whole thing behind a flag, or drop it if this is more surface than you want in the template — I'd rather ask than guess.\n",
        "html_url": "https://github.com/vercel/chatbot/pull/1541",
